-- title:
--   Lemma 2, p. 19 — for the ϵ-DeGroot process, ⟨µ^W, x^t⟩ is a martingale
-- statement:
--   Consider the $\epsilon$-DeGroot process, i.e. the random action process with probability-matching decision rule $\sigma(x)=x$ and belief recursion (1), on a network with weight matrix $W$ (row-stochastic, irreducible, $w_{ii}>0$) and centrality measure $\mu^W$ (a probability vector with $\mu^WW=\mu^W$). Let $\epsilon\in(0,1]$ and $\mathbf x^1\in[0,1]^m$. Then the centrality-weighted average belief
--   $$
--   M_t=\langle\mu^W,\mathbf x^t\rangle=\sum_{i\in V}\mu^W_i x_i^t
--   $$
--   is a martingale with respect to the filtration $(\mathcal F_t)$ generated by the action coins of the periods before $t$.
--
--   This martingale is the basic tool of the analysis of the $\epsilon$-DeGroot process: its limit identifies the probability of agreement on action $1$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The paper's filtration is generated by the beliefs $\{\mathbf x^\tau\}_{\tau\le t}$; the coin filtration used here contains it, so the statement is at least as strong (a martingale for a larger filtration, to which $M_t$ is adapted, is a martingale for the smaller one). Lean time $0$ is the paper's period $1$.
-- source:
--   Arieli, Babichenko & Mueller-Frank, Naive Learning Through Probability Over-Matching, SSRN working paper 3338015 (version of August 3, 2020), p. 19, Lemma 2

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_NaiveLearn_OverMatch_Model

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology

namespace NaiveLearn.OverMatch

/-- Lemma 2, p. 19: for the `ε`-DeGroot process (probability matching, `σ = id`) the centrality-
weighted average belief `⟨µ^W, x^t⟩ = Σ_i µ_i x_i^t` is a martingale. The filtration is generated
by the coins `U_0, …, U_{t−1}`, which contains `σ(x^0, …, x^t)`. -/
theorem lemma_2 {m : ℕ} (W : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin m) ℝ) (μ : Fin m → ℝ)
    (hW : IsWeightMatrix W) (hμ : IsCentrality W μ) (ε : ℝ) (hε : ε ∈ Set.Ioc (0 : ℝ) 1)
    (x1 : Fin m → ℝ) (hx1 : ∀ i, x1 i ∈ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1) :
    Martingale (fun t U => ∑ i, μ i * beliefAct id ε W x1 U t i) (coinFiltration m)
      (coinLaw m) := by sorry

end NaiveLearn.OverMatch
